{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-g1-reviewer-fable-32","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:aec630c60b86af0d2a96","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"For this submission only: the selected result (exact two-sided binomial test, 15 warm choices in 20 releases, p = 0.0414, reported as evidence of a warm-arm preference) treats all 20 CSV rows as independent Bernoulli trials, but the declared independent unit is salamander_id and only 5 animals exist, each contributing 4 repeated releases. The observed data show strong within-animal clustering (three animals chose warm 4/4, one chose cool 3/4), and any animal-level summary (3 warm-preferring vs 1 cool-preferring animals, 1 tied) is nowhere near significance at the 5% level. The stated statistical conclusion is therefore not supported by an analysis that respects the declared independent unit.","root_cause":"The workflow tallies warm choices across every row of data/input.csv and submits the pooled count directly to scipy.stats.binomtest(n_warm, n_total, 0.5), a procedure whose null distribution assumes each of the 20 rows is an independent trial. Because each of the 5 salamanders (the declared independent unit, salamander_id) is entered 4 times, within-animal correlation in arm choice inflates the effective sample size from 5 to 20 and shrinks the p-value; the code never groups by salamander_id or otherwise models the repeated entries.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares salamander_id as the independent-unit column while a row is a single release of one animal on one day.","observed_value":"Independent unit column: salamander_id","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":24,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"One row is: one Y-maze release of one salamander on one day, recording the arm it entered first and the latency to that entry\nIndependent unit column: salamander_id"}]},{"description":"The description states each of the 5 animals was released 4 times, so the same five salamanders account for all twenty rows — repeated entries of the independent unit.","observed_value":"5 animals x 4 releases = 20 rows","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":6,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"Each animal was released into the start box four times, once per\nday on four consecutive days, giving twenty scored releases in total."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":21,"end_line":22,"quoted_text":"Because every animal appears on four separate lines, the same five salamanders\naccount for all twenty lines in the file."}]},{"description":"The workflow pools warm-choice counts over all rows without any grouping by salamander_id.","observed_value":"n_warm summed over all 20 rows","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":29,"end_line":30,"quoted_text":"n_total = len(releases)\n    n_warm = sum(1 for row in releases if row[\"arm_chosen\"] == WARM_LABEL)"}]},{"description":"The pooled counts are passed to an exact binomial test, a row-independent procedure whose null assumes 20 independent Bernoulli trials.","observed_value":"binomtest(15, 20, 0.5) -> p = 0.0414","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":71,"end_line":71,"quoted_text":"outcome = binomtest(n_warm, n_total, NULL_P, alternative=\"two-sided\")"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly states that every scored release is entered as one independent Bernoulli trial and bases its significance claim on the pooled 20-release test.","observed_value":"p = 0.0414 from pooled releases","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":6,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"or cool). Every scored release is entered as one independent Bernoulli trial and\nthe pooled counts are submitted to an exact two-sided binomial test against the\nno-preference null of p = 0.5 (scipy.stats.binomtest)."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":20,"end_line":23,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial test, 15 warm-arm choices in 20 scored releases, null p = 0.5: proportion warm = 0.750, p = 0.0414.\n\nThe pooled releases depart from indifference at the conventional 5% threshold,\nso the assay is reported as evidence of a warm-arm preference."}]},{"description":"The raw data show strong within-animal clustering: SAL-01, SAL-02, and SAL-03 chose warm on all four releases, while SAL-04 chose cool on three of four; rows from the same animal are visibly not independent.","observed_value":{"SAL-01_warm":"4/4","SAL-02_warm":"4/4","SAL-03_warm":"4/4","SAL-04_warm":"1/4","SAL-05_warm":"2/4"},"source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"T01,SAL-01,1,warm,71\nT02,SAL-01,2,warm,58\nT03,SAL-01,3,warm,64\nT04,SAL-01,4,warm,49"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":14,"end_line":17,"quoted_text":"T13,SAL-04,1,cool,44\nT14,SAL-04,2,cool,51\nT15,SAL-04,3,cool,39\nT16,SAL-04,4,warm,57"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description itself says 'One trial is: one row', which could be read as authorizing row-level trials; however, this defines the row semantics, not statistical independence, and the same description names salamander_id as the independent-unit column.","observed_value":"One trial is: one row","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":26,"end_line":26,"quoted_text":"One trial is: one row"}]},{"description":"The design swapped warm and cool arms between days, which rules out a fixed side preference as the driver of the pattern; this mitigates side-bias confounding but does nothing to remove the dependence among the four releases of the same animal.","observed_value":"arm sides swapped across days","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":9,"end_line":11,"quoted_text":"The warm and cool arms were\nswapped between days so that a fixed side preference could not by itself produce\nthe pattern of choices."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to defend the pooled analysis two ways. (1) If within-animal releases were effectively independent, per-animal choice sequences should look like independent coin flips of a shared warm probability; instead three of five animals are 4/4 warm and one is 3/4 cool, extreme clustering by salamander_id that contradicts the row-independence assumption on the observed data themselves. (2) I re-ran the inference at the declared independent unit: classifying each animal by majority choice gives 3 warm-preferring, 1 cool-preferring, 1 tied (SAL-05 at 2/2); an exact two-sided binomial/sign test on the 4 non-tied animals gives p = 0.625, and even the most favorable animal-level reading (4 of 5 animals warm-leaning) gives p = 0.375. I also verified the reported pooled value, binomtest(15, 20, 0.5) two-sided p ≈ 0.0414, so the report's numbers are internally correct but the significance claim vanishes under any unit-respecting analysis. Both defenses fail, so the issue stands.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}